/-!
# Stilt

A Lean model of the core calculus of Stilt: its syntax, methods and their
receivers included, typing, the law of exclusivity, a semantics with two models
of memory, the soundness statements of the plan, the optimality of the Reference's
rule on `inout` arguments, the modes of modes.fact-not-type, which change nothing a run
observes, a model of memory in which copies into bindings share storage and the writes that
modes.shared-writes calls unique write in place, with programs the checker accepts whose
shared writes it copies first, as copies show, the views of views.what-view-is, whose windows and copies agree under the law of
exclusivity, the writable views of projections.writable-views, whose windows and copies
written back agree under it too, descending recursion under facts.descending-self-calls, which
ends, the member types and kinds of traits.member-types, which coherence makes mean
one type and instantiation keeps well kinded, the rigid type parameters of
polymorphism.rigid-types, which an argument checked with them keeps at every instantiation,
the record bounds of records.record-bounds, through which a read stays well typed at every
record that meets them, the spreads of variadics.spread-expressions, which map a pack of any
length, the empty one included, to a tuple of as many elements, the
hygienic expansions of macros.hygiene, whose binders capture no name of the user's,
the one-shot handlers of handlers.resume-at-most-once, which a checked program never resumes
twice, and whose rows leave no operation performed without a handler, the
`when`s of shared.when, which take all their values at once and so never
deadlock, the calls of std.par, which end alike run at the same time as one after another
(par.sequential-meaning), the cycles of cycles.can-form-cycle, which no value of a type that
can't form one is ever on, the folds of par.fold-tree, whose every schedule gives the value of
the tree fixed by the list's length, and the four interactions of handlers.call-that-handler-ends to
tasks.continuations-stay-in-task: calls a handler ends and `when`s whose names may reach one value
leave the same store copied or written in place, operations given values
write nothing of the code they suspend, no value is held by two running
`when`s, and no continuation leaves its task.
`README.md` describes what it covers.
-/
